 "overview": "This file (decimal 894.4038/10-549, File 1769) consists of Despatch No. 695, dated October 5, 1949, from the Office of the United States Political Adviser for Japan in Tokyo to the Department of State, forwarding copies of Information Bulletin No. 15, entitled 'Western Music in Japan,' prepared on September 29, 1949, by the Public Information Office of General Headquarters, Far East Command (GHQ FEC). The enclosure presents a comprehensive survey of the development, wartime suppression, and post-World War II resurgence of Western musical culture in Japan. Tracing roots back to Meiji-era initiatives by Shuji Izawa and American educator Luther Whiting Mason that created the Tokyo Academy of Music, the bulletin covers the founding of the Japan Philharmonic Orchestra under Hidemaro Konoe and Joseph Rosenstock, wartime censorship by the Nazi-patterned Japan Musical Culture Association, and postwar cultural reorientation efforts led by SCAP's Civil Information and Education Section (CIE). It highlights the introduction of dozens of contemporary American composers through live concerts, nationwide radio broadcasts, and CIE Information Center record libraries, while surveying the revival of Japanese opera companies (Fujiwara and Nagato Miho), ballet, the war-damaged phonograph recording industry (Japan Victor and Japan Columbia), and the nationwide craze for American dance music.",
